Subject: [PATCH] loot: a roll window that opens is answered

WHY THIS PATCH EXISTS

A party of five runs Group Loot with the threshold at Uncommon. Everything
below the threshold distributes normally. Everything at or above it opens a
roll window that NOBODY answers - not need, not greed, not pass. Sixty seconds
later the roll expires and the item is gone.

THE ONLY PACKET THAT SAYS "A ROLL WINDOW OPENED" IS THROWN AWAY

There are two packets with similar names and they are not interchangeable:

    SMSG_LOOT_START_ROLL   Group::SendLootStartRoll - the window OPENING, sent
                           once per roll to every member whose vote is still
                           NOT_EMITED_YET. This is the one that starts a clock.
    SMSG_LOOT_ROLL         Group::SendLootRoll - the "somebody has rolled"
                           broadcast, emitted only AFTER a vote (or an
                           auto-pass) already exists.

The module binds the first one like this:

    botOutgoingPacketHandlers.AddHandler(SMSG_LOOT_START_ROLL, "master loot roll");

and `"master loot roll"` is a name that NO trigger context registers. Grepped
every NamedObjectContext<Trigger> in the module - base, chat, world packet,
every class context, every dungeon and raid context. The string appears exactly
four times and not one of them is a trigger creator:

    LootRollAction.h                 the action's own name
    WorldPacketActionContext.h       the ACTION creator
    WorldPacketHandlerStrategy.cpp   a TriggerNode asking for the name
    PlayerbotAI.cpp                  the packet binding above

Both consumers of that name fail closed, and both fail SILENTLY:

    ExternalEventHelper::HandlePacket   Trigger* trigger = GetTrigger(name);
                                        if (!trigger) return;      <- dropped
    Engine::ProcessTriggers             if (!trigger) continue;    <- skipped

So the packet is received, queued by PacketHandlingHelper::AddPacket (which
only queues opcodes that have a handler name, so it does queue this one), and
then discarded one step later. MasterLootRollAction is unreachable code and the
TriggerNode that asks for it has never once resolved.

WHAT WAS LEFT DRIVING THE VOTE

Exactly one thing: a random poll.

    triggers.push_back(new TriggerNode("very often", { NextAction("loot roll", relevance) }));

`"very often"` is RandomTrigger(botAI, "often", 3):

    bool RandomTrigger::IsActive()
    {
        if (getMSTime() - lastCheck < sPlayerbotAIConfig.repeatDelay)
            return false;
        lastCheck = getMSTime();
        int32 k = (int32)(probability / sPlayerbotAIConfig.randomChangeMultiplier);
        if (k < 1) k = 1;
        return (rand() % k) == 0;
    }

That is the right shape for a chore with no deadline - repair, sell greys. A
loot roll has a deadline: the sixty seconds the packet itself declares. A
repeated coin flip against a wall clock is not a mechanism for meeting one.

WHY THE PREVIOUS ATTEMPT AT THIS DID NOT WORK

The patch this one replaces bound SMSG_LOOT_ROLL to `"loot roll"`. That packet
is the "somebody has rolled" broadcast quoted above. In a party where nobody
manages to roll, it is never sent, so it can never bootstrap the first vote.
Verified against the pinned core rather than assumed: Group::SendLootRoll is
only ever called from CountRollVote (after a vote is recorded), from
CountTheRoll, and from the auto-pass sweep in GroupLoot for members who fail
CanRollOnItem. None of those happen at window-open for a party that is
eligible to roll. That patch was built on the belief that SMSG_LOOT_ROLL was
the roll-opening packet. It is not, so it is replaced here rather than left in
the tree to mislead the next reader.

THE FIX

Bind SMSG_LOOT_START_ROLL to `"loot roll"` - a name that IS registered
(WorldPacketTriggerContext::loot_roll -> WorldPacketTrigger) and that already
has a TriggerNode in every one of a bot's engines, because
WorldPacketHandlerStrategy's constructor pushes `"loot roll"` into `supported`
and PassThroughStrategy::InitTriggers turns every supported name into a node at
relevance 100.

Traced end to end before writing it:

    WorldSession::SendPacket -> sScriptMgr->OnPlayerbotPacketSent, which runs
        BEFORE the `if (!m_Socket) return;` - so a character with a real client
        attached gets this too, not only a spawned bot
    -> PlayerbotAI::HandleBotOutgoingPacket, default case -> AddPacket
    -> PacketHandlingHelper::Handle drains the WHOLE queue, not one per tick
    -> ExternalEventHelper::HandlePacket -> WorldPacketTrigger::ExternalEvent
       sets triggered = true
    -> UpdateAIInternal runs botOutgoingPacketHandlers.Handle immediately
       before DoNextAction, so the fire lands on the SAME tick
    -> Engine::ProcessTriggers -> MultiplyAndPush at relevance 100
    -> Engine::DoNextAction: Queue::Peek returns the HIGHEST relevance basket,
       and both minimal-tick filters are `< 100`, so a relevance-100 loot roll
       survives a minimal tick and cannot be starved behind a lower one
    -> LootRollAction::Execute, which ignores the packet payload entirely and
       rescans group->GetRolls() for every roll where this bot's own vote is
       still NOT_EMITED_YET, then calls Group::CountRollVote directly.

Ignoring the payload is a feature here, not a shortcut: one fire answers every
open roll, and a second fire is idempotent because an answered vote is skipped.
Queue::Push also coalesces by action name, so a packet fire landing in the same
tick as the random poll cannot double-push.

The random poll is left in place. It is not wrong, only insufficient on its
own for something with a countdown.

WHAT THIS DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO

It adds no selfbot or IsRandomBot branch, and it removes none. There is no such
gate on this path: IsRealPlayer(player) is `player && !GET_PLAYERBOT_AI(player)`,
so a selfbot's master (itself, which has bot AI) reads as NOT a real player and
lands on the same branch as a spawned bot everywhere this path checks. The two
selfbot mentions near this code (StoreLootAction's isUseful, and
ItemUsageValue's master-quest deferral) both make a selfbot MORE permissive,
not less.

It does not touch LootRollAction's vote logic. That logic is already correct
after 0008 and 0013; it was simply never asked to run on time.

It removes the "master loot roll" TriggerNode rather than registering a trigger
for it. MasterLootRollAction is strictly weaker than the action already wired
here: it parses one roll out of the packet and votes with CalculateRollVote
alone, bypassing the armour-token branch, the recipe branch, and the
weapon/armour branch that 0008 and 0013 exist to get right. Wiring it up would
have re-introduced the bugs those two patches fixed, on the deterministic path.

NOT VERIFIED LIVE. This is traced from source against the pinned trees and
proven to apply and compile; it has not been watched resolving a real roll on a
running world. That is the acceptance test, and it is on the linked issue.

A NOTE ON THE COMMENT PREFIX. This uses `// local:` rather than the `// infra:`
some older patches use. These files are copied byte-identical into a public
repository whose policy forbids naming a private repository anywhere in it, in
any form, and that older prefix names one. `// local:` carries the same meaning -
this is our change, not upstream - without doing that. Do not 'correct' it back
for consistency.

